<SiteLayout title="Privacy · You’re Flocked" description="Why You’re Flocked asks for “Always” location, what leaves your phone, what stays on it, and where the road data comes from. The plain-English privacy page, with a link to the full document.">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-heading"><h1>What the app knows.<br /><em>And what it doesn’t.</em></h1><p>This is the plain-English version. The full privacy document on GitHub lists every network request the app makes and how to check that list against the source code. If the two ever disagree, the full document wins.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href={site.privacy} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the full document <Icon name="external" /></a><a class="text-link" href={site.discussions} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ask a question <Icon name="external" /></a></div></header>
    <div class="hosting-overview"><div><h2>The short version.</h2><p>No account, no sign-up, nothing that identifies you. No analytics, telemetry, crash reporting or ads. Routes are planned on your phone, so where you start and where you’re going never go anywhere. And the app keeps no record of where you’ve been.</p></div><div class="hosting-flow" aria-label="Your location is compared with camera positions on your phone, and the alert comes from your phone"><span>Your location</span><Icon /><span>Compared on your phone</span><Icon /><span>Alert from your phone</span></div></div>
    <div class="document-layout">
      <aside class="contents"><h2>On this page</h2><nav aria-label="Privacy sections"><a href="#permissions">Why “Always” location</a><a href="#what-leaves">What leaves your phone</a><a href="#what-stays">What stays on your phone</a><a href="#tile-server">Where the road data comes from</a><a href="#reduce">Reducing your exposure</a><a href="#checking">Checking for yourself</a></nav><a class="text-link" href={site.privacy} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Full privacy document <Icon name="external" /></a></aside>
      <article class="prose guide">
        <section id="permissions"><h2>Why it asks for “Always” location.</h2><p>The first thing you’ll see is a location prompt, and it asks for “Always”. Here’s why. Alerts are the point of the app, and they’re only useful if they work while you’re driving with the phone in your pocket. iOS calls that background access. To do it, the app reads your location continuously and compares it with the camera positions already stored on your phone. That comparison happens on the phone and never leaves it.</p><p>If you choose “While Using” instead, the map and the route planner still work. You just won’t get alerts unless the app is open.</p><p>The notification prompt is for those alerts. They’re created on your phone, not sent to it. There’s no push server.</p></section>
        <section id="what-leaves"><h2>What leaves your phone.</h2><p>The app isn’t entirely offline. Camera data and map imagery come from other people’s services, and those requests say something about where you’re looking. Like any internet request, they also show that server your IP address. Here’s who gets what, most revealing first.</p>
          <h3>Apple Maps</h3><p>The map imagery is Apple’s, so Apple sees which part of the map you’re viewing. When you search for a destination in the route planner, Apple also receives what you type and the map region.</p>
          <h3>OpenStreetMap servers</h3><p>Speed camera data comes from OpenStreetMap through community-run “Overpass” servers. The app never sends your position. It asks for a cell of a fixed grid, one degree of latitude by one of longitude, about 111 km by 85 km across most of the US. The grid is the same for everyone, so every phone in a cell sends exactly the same request. Once a cell is loaded it stays on your phone and isn’t asked for again for a day, so on a drive a new request goes out roughly when you cross into the next cell. Planning a route loads the cells around your start and destination the same way: these servers learn which cells, never the two points. If DeFlock is unavailable, the same request covers every camera type. If you switch off “Show Speed Cameras” and DeFlock is up, nothing is sent here at all.</p>
          <h3>DeFlock</h3><p>License plate reader locations come from DeFlock in large tiles, each covering a 20-degree slice of the globe. Asking for one reveals roughly which part of the world you’re looking at, not where you are. It happens when the app loads a grid cell and the tile covering it is missing or out of date.</p>
          <h3>The tile server</h3><p>When you download a region pack for routing, the server learns which state-sized region you asked for and nothing else: not your position, your route or your destination. It only happens while a download is running. <a href="#tile-server">More on this below.</a></p>
          <h3>ALPRWatch</h3><p>Off by default. If you switch on “Show Suspected Locations”, the app downloads one file from ALPRWatch. The request says nothing about you.</p>
          <h3>Links you tap</h3><p>The camera detail screen and Settings have ordinary web links, and nothing is sent until you tap one. “View on OpenStreetMap” and “View on DeFlock” open the page for that camera, which tells the site which camera you looked at, a hint about where you are. They’re there for checking and correcting map data, and the choice is yours each time.</p></section>
        <section id="what-stays"><h2>What stays on your phone.</h2><ul><li><strong>Region packs:</strong> the road data you downloaded for routing. It describes roads, not you.</li><li><strong>Cached camera records:</strong> so the map and alerts work without asking again. They describe cameras, not you.</li><li><strong>Your settings:</strong> alert distance, filters and map preferences.</li></ul><p>That’s the whole list. No location log, no visit history, no route history. Delete the app and all of it goes with it.</p></section>
        <section id="tile-server"><h2>Where the road data comes from.</h2><p>Routing on your phone needs road data on your phone, and that has to be downloaded from somewhere. By default it comes from <a href={site.tiles} target="_blank" rel="noopener"><code>tiles.flckd.app</code></a>: a bucket of static files on Cloudflare with a domain name in front. I upload new packs about once a month. There’s no web server, no application and no machine of mine anywhere in the path, so nothing of mine can log the request, be breached or be subpoenaed.</p><p>Cloudflare does see your IP address and which region you asked for. That’s a coarse hint about an area you’re interested in, and a real disclosure, which is why it’s here. I haven’t turned on any of Cloudflare’s logging or analytics products; what Cloudflare keeps for itself is governed by its policy, not mine.</p><p>You don’t have to use it. The app lets you point at any tile server, including one at home, and it never quietly falls back to mine if yours fails. You can also sideload a pack, or download it once over a VPN. After that, routing needs no network at all.</p><a class="text-link" href="/hosting/">How to host the road data yourself <Icon /></a></section>
        <section id="reduce"><h2>Ways to reduce your exposure.</h2><ul><li>Leave “Show Suspected Locations” off unless you want it. It’s off by default.</li><li>The speed camera request goes out as soon as the app knows which grid cell you’re in, unless that cell was loaded in the last day. Panning the map to a new cell sends that cell; it doesn’t take back the first one. Switching off “Show Speed Cameras” stops the request entirely.</li><li>Use a VPN to hide your IP address from the OpenStreetMap servers and from whichever tile server you use.</li><li>Once an area’s cameras are cached and its region pack is downloaded, both alerts and route planning work with no network at all.</li></ul></section>
        <section id="checking"><h2>Checking for yourself.</h2><p>You don’t have to take any of this on trust. The full document names the handful of source files that make network requests and gives the commands to confirm there are no others, including one that shows the routing code never touches the network.</p><p>There’s no separate changelog for the privacy document, either: every change to it is a commit in the repository, in the open.</p><a class="button secondary" href={site.privacy} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the full privacy document <Icon name="external" /></a></section>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
